/* Settings › Performance › Disable glass (or Auto mode, when the frame rate stays too low). BakeryAppManager adds this
   style sheet to the app root, and takes it off again: nothing else changes. Every blurred backdrop stops blurring and keeps
   its tint: the frosted glass (the scrolling pages' cover and the nav's bar), the dialogs' scrims and the Lucky Bag's
   caption and results. LEVEL UP!'s halftone (a custom filter) stays.

   Measured on 2026-10-16: the scrolling pages' glass blur alone took Shops from 14 to 105 ms of GPU on an iPhone 12 Pro
   and from 22 to 218 ms on a Galaxy S10, whose nav bar blur also cost about 24 ms. Two classes outrank the pages' own
   one-class rules; Disable all filters (#app *) still wins over everything. */

/* Glass that doesn't blur is nearly opaque instead, so what scrolls under it doesn't show through sharply. */
.be-app .be-glass {
    background-color: var(--be-frost-solid);
}

.be-app .be-glass,
.be-app .be-confirm__scrim,
.be-app .be-recipes__scrim,
.be-app .be-lucky-bag__banner-text,
.be-app .be-lucky-bag__results {
    backdrop-filter: none;
}
